Traveler's Diarrhea (Enterotoxigenic E. coli)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Sudden onset watery stools, Recent travel to endemic region, Severe episodic abdominal cramping

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-047
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of uncontrollable watery diarrhea and severe abdominal cramps following international travel, resulting in marked weakness and inability to maintain daily activities.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, explosive onset of severe watery diarrhea leading to orthostatic dizziness upon standing. The patient's recent return from a developing region indicated a likely infectious etiology. An urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate hemodynamic stability, exclude invasive dysenteric pathogens, and provide targeted antibiotic and antimotility therapy to prevent severe dehydration prior to routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Unstoppable watery diarrhea, severe stomach cramps, and feeling extremely faint. Patient states, I just got back from Bali two days ago, and suddenly my stomach cramped violently and I have been chained to the toilet passing pure water ever since.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced abruptly 12 hours ago with a sudden onset of frequent, unformed, watery loose stools. The patient confirms a history of recent travel to an endemic developing region (Southeast Asia) returning within the past two weeks. The diarrhea is accompanied by severe, episodic abdominal cramping that causes the patient to double over in pain. There is an urgent, precipitous need to defecate (tenesmus). The patient reports moderate nausea without active vomiting. There is a low-grade fever and excessive abdominal bloating and flatulence. The patient complains of orthostatic dizziness and a deeply dry mouth, reflecting moderate volume loss.
Functional Status: Confined to the immediate vicinity of the bathroom due to unpredictable, urgent bowel movements.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of unobserved syncope due to volume depletion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg (Supine) -> 95/65 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75.0 Kg
General: Appears fatigued, flushed, and is actively clutching their abdomen during crampy spasms.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is present. A mild orthostatic drop in blood pressure is objectively confirmed, directly correlating with the reported dizziness. Respiratory: Lungs are clear. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is firmly established. There is a sudden onset of watery stools (visualized). There is a confirmed recent travel to an endemic region. The patient exhibits severe episodic abdominal cramping with diffuse, mild lower abdominal tenderness but zero rebound, guarding, or rigidity to suggest a surgical abdomen. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Integumentary: Examination reveals dry oral mucous membranes and slightly decreased skin turgor, confirming moderate clinical dehydration.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Bathroom access is unimpeded.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took two over-the-counter Loperamide tablets earlier with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Traveler's Diarrhea (Enterotoxigenic E. coli) - Status: Acute, localized infection
Differentials:

Shigellosis / Invasive Bacterial Dysentery - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of gross blood or heavy mucus in the stool, and the absence of a high, spiking fever with severe systemic toxicity.

Giardiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyperacute, explosive onset of watery diarrhea, whereas Giardia typically presents with an insidious onset of extremely foul-smelling, greasy stools and profound chronic bloating.

Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Diffuse, crampy pain clearly associated with massive diarrhea, completely lacking focal right lower quadrant peritonitis or rigidity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic Traveler's Diarrhea, characterized by acute, massive secretory fluid loss into the gut lumen triggered by enterotoxin-producing bacteria (likely ETEC) acquired during recent travel. The profound frequency of the watery stools has led to moderate intravascular volume depletion, evidenced by tachycardia and orthostatic dizziness. Delaying assessment until the standard in-hours period would have left the patient suffering from debilitating symptoms and at risk of worsening hypovolemia. Urgent after-hours assessment confirmed the hemodynamic stability, explicitly ruled out invasive bloody dysentery (which would contraindicate antimotility agents), and allowed for the immediate initiation of a short-course macrolide antibiotic and high-dose loperamide to rapidly halt the fluid loss and resolve the acute illness. The expected investigation results include negative routine stool cultures for invasive pathogens (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter) with rapid clinical resolution on empiric short-course azithromycin that would assist in confirming the diagnosis, usually making stool tests unnecessary.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Azithromycin 500mg PO daily for 3 days to eradicate the presumed pathogen. Advised the continuation of Loperamide 2mg PO after every loose stool (maximum 8 tablets daily) to halt transit. Initiated aggressive oral rehydration therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To replace the fluid you are rapidly losing, commit to drinking 250ml of an oral rehydration solution precisely after every single bout of diarrhea. Keep a tally sheet next to the toilet. When you mark off a glass and subsequently notice your dizziness fading when you stand, consciously acknowledge that your deliberate hydration is directly correcting your low blood pressure, reinforcing your strict adherence to the routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 72 hours only if symptoms persist or if bloody diarrhea or high fever develops.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the nearest Emergency Department or call 000 if you begin passing bright red blood or dark tarry stools, if your abdominal pain becomes severe, constant, and rigid, or if you become entirely unable to stand up without fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumptive diagnosis based on classic travel history; failure to improve requires stool testing for parasites or invasive bacteria.
Clinical Course: A bacteria from your travel is producing a toxin that forces your bowel to violently flush water. The prescribed antibiotic will kill the bacteria rapidly, and the anti-diarrhea medication will immediately slow the bowel to allow fluid absorption. Symptoms should drastically improve within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not drink plain tap water or sugary sodas. Plain water will not replace the specific salts you have lost, and high sugar drinks will actually pull more water into your bowel, severely worsening the diarrhea.

